WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.591
A correct security check is valuable only
if every relevant path applies it.

2
00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:07.356
Place enforcement according to
responsibility.

3
00:00:07.436 --> 00:00:11.718
Authentication middleware verifies
credentials and builds trusted context.

4
00:00:11.798 --> 00:00:14.228
Boundary validators parse untrusted input.

5
00:00:14.308 --> 00:00:19.102
Application services and policy functions
make operation-specific authorization and

6
00:00:19.182 --> 00:00:20.332
workflow decisions.

7
00:00:20.412 --> 00:00:22.458
Repositories constrain data access,

8
00:00:22.538 --> 00:00:26.565
while transactions and database
constraints protect shared invariants.

9
00:00:26.645 --> 00:00:29.330
Not every control belongs in Express
middleware,

10
00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.756
and one enormous middleware component can
make critical decisions difficult to

11
00:00:33.836 --> 00:00:34.220
trace.

12
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.176
Prefer small, named contracts with secure
defaults.

13
00:00:37.256 --> 00:00:40.964
A policy such as canExportReport
communicates the subject, action,

14
00:00:41.044 --> 00:00:42.578
and resource being decided.

15
00:00:42.658 --> 00:00:47.132
A service that requires AuthContext
prevents callers from conveniently omitting

16
00:00:47.212 --> 00:00:47.788
identity.

17
00:00:47.868 --> 00:00:51.064
An allowlisted update type makes writable
fields visible.

18
00:00:51.144 --> 00:00:54.468
Centralize repeated rules so alternate
routes cannot drift,

19
00:00:54.548 --> 00:00:58.383
but keep invocation explicit enough for a
reviewer to see enforcement.

20
00:00:58.463 --> 00:01:01.979
When context is missing or no rule grants
access, deny by default.

21
00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:04.296
Test the server as an adversarial client.

22
00:01:04.376 --> 00:01:07.572
Send requests with no authentication and
malformed input,

23
00:01:07.652 --> 00:01:10.273
then valid input with insufficient
permission.

24
00:01:10.353 --> 00:01:14.380
Attempt cross-user and cross-tenant
identifiers, server-owned fields,

25
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.359
invalid transitions, repeated submissions,
and mixed-scope batches.

26
00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:21.763
Exercise every route that reaches the same
sensitive action.

27
00:01:21.843 --> 00:01:26.318
Direct API tests matter because browser
tests may preserve the UI restriction that

28
00:01:26.398 --> 00:01:27.548
needs to be bypassed.

29
00:01:27.628 --> 00:01:30.952
Include concurrent tests for
timing-dependent invariants,

30
00:01:31.032 --> 00:01:34.228
and assert both the response and protected
database state.

31
00:01:34.308 --> 00:01:39.039
Observability supports detection and
investigation without becoming a new exposure.

32
00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:43.146
Record authorization denials, repeated
sensitive-operation attempts,

33
00:01:43.226 --> 00:01:46.294
invalid transitions, and unusual
validation failures.

34
00:01:46.374 --> 00:01:49.123
Include stable request, subject, tenant,
action,

35
00:01:49.203 --> 00:01:53.102
and outcome identifiers where policy
permits, but do not log passwords,

36
00:01:53.182 --> 00:01:57.592
session tokens, authorization headers,
secrets, or unnecessary personal data.

37
00:01:57.672 --> 00:02:00.421
Protect logs from untrusted log-forging
content.

38
00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:03.761
Metrics can reveal failing integrations or
active probing,

39
00:02:03.841 --> 00:02:08.060
while focused audit records show who
requested a sensitive change and what the

40
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.035
server decided.

41
00:02:09.115 --> 00:02:11.864
Consistent enforcement, bypass-oriented
tests,

42
00:02:11.944 --> 00:02:14.820
and disciplined telemetry form an
assurance system.
